 image designed to  completely and utterly destroy us from  the inside and that's what it did  but before we get into that this chapter  was really great in terms of expanding  the field of cursed corpses like we've  had this discussion before when mcmurra  fought against panda but there is indeed  something very special about mr pander  as well as the rest of janka's creations  there's a pretty in-depth explanation  around the end of the chapter stating  that creating an independent corpse  involves replicating soul information  and needing to house three of these soul  drives in one vessel which eventually  evolves into a self-sustaining cursed  energy machine and this immediately  contributes a ton to a conversation that  jujutsu kaisen has been having since  i think the dawn of the series which is  what is a soul how do we quantify  it and how is it tied to the body is it  tied to the body mahito's answer is that  the soul was paramount and controls the  shape of the body meanwhile the  phenomena of toji went on to suggest  that the body and soul can't really be  separate and even the suggestion of a  body could go on to generate  its own soul and now here we are with  yaga who claims to be able to replicate  soul-based entities on purely physical  information without  impacting the shape of the new body  every time soul information is brought  up we get a very simple explanation  attached to it however each one of these  explanations of what souls are and how  to manipulate them tend to contradict  the previous idea which is great because  it is a complicated issue and in the end  who even knows if a soul  even exists i don't you don't unless you  do  if you do let me know in the comments  i'm very curious but i tell you what  this whole chapter gives new meaning to  the term  cursed corpse i've always been intrigued  by that title because i don't think  we've ever seen an actual corpse used  for this technique however in yaga's  case specifically the corpse does not  refer to the remnants of the body but  rather the remnants of the soul which  was gathered from the body and just  yeah i find it really interesting  especially when you think about the  example that was given in chapter 147  takuru the cute cartoony  corpse with a tie firstly there's an  interesting question of how  ethical it is to seemingly quite  literally create a copy of someone  else's soul and that's a question we're  sort of beginning to tackle in the real  world right now with celebrity deep  fakes and using the likeness of dead  people to perform in films and  advertising it's a really weird  and uncomfortable question but here it's  taken to a whole new level because as  heartwarming as this  sort of is part of me does feel really  uncomfortable with copying the soul of  that woman's dead son like in theory  there is no harm in it for this  particular situation  because it was used to give the woman a  reason to live there's just something  that  ah doesn't feel quite right which is  pretty perfectly jujutsu  i don't think it's supposed to feel  right because even our good guys in this  series are ultimately  dealing with very morally questionable  powers across the board and this is just  the latest iteration it's bigger than  that though because this also speaks to  literally being able to create life  because this isn't takaru it's like an  amalgamation of three people housed  within a single vessel so in effect this  becomes an entirely new existence  just molded from the characteristics of  the other three but i think the true  tragedy really sets in when you realize  that panda or at least part of panda was  in fact yanka's son it's not explicitly  stated but the implication i get is that  just like this woman  yaga's son died under tragic  circumstances so his soul was replicated  and is now contributing to the existence  that is the glorious pandar so that's  quite tragically touching here because  obviously yaga could heavily identify  with the woman's situation and even  though he technically understands the  existence of both panda and takaru he  still cannot help but see  panda as his son that took a while to  really sink in with me but i think about  that panel now where yoga claims that  he's going to see his son  and it's tough to read just like a lot  of this chapter  especially in retrospect and you know  i'm very much not  looking forward to seeing this animated  however many years that may take from  now because with voice acting and music  this could be a properly tear-generating  and soul-destroying moment rather  ironically and definitely the ending  with panda as well this stupid panel  ah even pandas cry i hate this panel  because it does exactly what i said by  punching you in the gut after setting up  this really sweet bond and i want to  emphasize for the people who  take things very literally i don't  actually hate this panel it's very  beautiful  and emotional but i hate looking at it  because it generates  many many feels while run panda we had a  very intriguing exchange with  gaku ganji someone who i now despise  with all of my being  despite how damn cool he looks with that  guitar but panda really laid some  foundations here with the whole human  behavior's don't bind panda speech and  his ability to look past  reactionary human inclinations in a  weird way panda represents  a level of evolution the sort of stuff  that jogo was spouting about an entirely  new breed of true humans panda is human  at his core  or cause plural but he exists on an  entirely different plane of concerns and  what can i say panda is the best of us  and good on akutami for taking the super  weird concept of a panda sorcerer and  turning it into a deeply emotional  journey it also screw akutami for doing  that having brought up  old mate gakuganji earlier something  else not to be understated in this  chapter  yaga's last words all he had to do in  order to live was reveal the formula for  crafting 